        S('E. Behavioral Testing and Formal Execution'),
        'Ribeiro et al. propose CheckList to expose model failures through behavioral testing beyond a single held-out accuracy value [7]. This motivates targeted tests for negation, boundaries, literal substitution and combinations of requirements. TheoryBERT keeps known product fixtures, a retired mixed challenge and a newly frozen comparison. A fixture is a regression obligation once it has influenced development. It cannot also serve as independent confirmation that a newly trained model generalizes.',
        'Classical automata and parsing methods supply the executable semantics after language analysis. Cornell\'s compiler notes explain total DFA transitions and the connection between regular specifications and lexical analysis [12]. NLTK documents incremental Earley chart parsing [11]. TheoryBERT uses these established principles to validate structures, simulate words and check derivations. The project contribution is the integration and evidence discipline, rather than a new DFA equivalence or general CFG parsing algorithm.',
        'Exact DFA equivalence uses product exploration. General CFG equivalence is not supplied. A parse verifies membership under one grammar, not all-word correspondence with English. Preserving an AST in symbolic intermediate code is likewise a bounded claim, without a full-language optimizer or target-machine execution.',
        S('F. Research Gap and Positioning'),
        'The local engineering gap is a consistent end-to-end contract between learned interpretation and an educational representation. A system can have a useful neural model but still display a stale graph. It can also have a correct symbolic renderer while silently constructing the language with handwritten English rules. TheoryBERT addresses this gap by passing explicit model-produced objects through formal checks and by drawing the resulting machine or derivation. The rendering system has no separate authority to invent a convenient default graph.',
        'The new experiment evaluates a specific hypothesis within that architecture: semantic plans and learned literal copying may improve the relation between a request and its final DFA while reducing trainable capacity. The comparison preserves the visual components, uses the same encoder lineage and records both old validation behavior and fresh wording performance. These controls narrow the interpretation of any observed gain. They do not isolate every possible difference, since the new supervision and output vocabulary also differ from the direct model.',
        'The approach combines established pretrained representations, intermediate semantics, formal constraints and behavioral testing. It claims no first invention, universal correctness or superiority to large language models. Human-authored evaluation, matched-data ablations and several seeds remain necessary.'
