        S('C. BERT Semantic Plan Decoder'),
        'The new head projects contextual BERT features from width 768 to 128. Twenty-four learned output queries attend through two Transformer decoder layers, with four attention heads and feed-forward width 256. A token classifier predicts operators, constants and termination. Separate start and end projections score literal boundaries against encoder tokens. A single-bit pattern uses a learned fixed output class; longer patterns use the learned copy action and token-span scores.',
        'The typed vocabulary includes universal and empty languages; prefix, suffix, substring and exact-word requirements; Boolean negation, conjunction and disjunction; count equality and bounds; and count or length modulo conditions. Numbers are restricted to 0 through 16. The compiler accepts binary literals up to 32 characters and at most 64 final states. The generated training catalogue covers patterns up to eight characters and moduli 2 through 8. Implementation capacity must not be confused with demonstrated training coverage.',
        'Training minimizes token cross-entropy plus weighted start/end cross-entropies at copy positions. Padding is excluded. Encoder weights are immutable and fingerprinted. A packed float16 token-feature cache stores real tokens only; batches are padded on demand. The head is selected using validation plan-and-copy exactness, followed by independent DFA validation gates. The test partition is excluded from feature extraction, gradient updates and checkpoint selection.',
        'At inference, a bounded beam tracks the argument types still required by the predicted prefix expression. It ranks up to three token alternatives for a type and up to two literal spans for copying, with beam width eight. Search uses relative neural score gaps and deduplicates completed canonical DFAs. The formal compiler rejects invalid arguments and bounds. There is no English keyword matcher, reference-guided plan construction or replacement of an incorrect prediction with a hand-constructed answer.',
