    pages.append(('Website and User Experience',[
        S('Workspaces and Technology'),
        'The primary interface is a React application built with Vite and served from frontend/dist by Starlette. React Router supplies workspaces and navigation. The source uses Lucide icons, Motion interactions and locally packaged fonts. A legacy Streamlit application remains in the repository, but the current local launcher serves the React product. The backend uses Python, PyTorch, Transformers, NLTK, NumPy and optional Manim rendering.',
        S('DFA Interaction'),
        'The user enters a language description and a binary test string. Learned mode invokes the active evaluated BERT checkpoint or an explicitly selected local checkpoint. Reference demonstrations, intentionally incorrect fixtures and manual DFA JSON are distinct modes with clear provenance. A benchmark example can supply its independent reference; a free-form request generally cannot. The interface shows either verified evidence or an explicitly unverified candidate preview.',
        'The visible graph is generated from actual states and transitions. Accepting-state rings, start markers and edge labels follow the formal object. Simulation highlights the current state and consumed input symbol. Input-only edits recompute the trace for the same machine, while a changed English requirement triggers a new prediction. A suffix-01 requirement produces a three-state minimal machine; suffix-0101 requires five. A longer test word can loop through those five states without creating extra graph nodes.',
        'Analysis panels derive transition tables, state visits, access strings, accepting completions and accepted/rejected examples from the candidate. These facts help inspect behavior but do not prove correspondence to English. A shortest formal counterexample is shown when an independent reference reveals a disagreement. Empty input is handled as epsilon and is not replaced by an arbitrary demonstration string.',
        S('Grammar and Compiler Interaction'),
        'The grammar workspace displays BERT-produced productions and parse outcomes for the current tokens. Input changes can reuse the same compatible BERT grammar result, then reparse the new sequence. Tree alternatives and derivation playback correspond to actual parser output. Arithmetic mode additionally presents lexemes, a parse structure, AST and symbolic IR. Unsupported grammar output or invalid source is reported without substituting a known fixture.',
        S('Presentation Preservation and Responsive Behavior'),
        'The current experiment keeps Diagram.jsx, layout.py, scenes.py, renderer.py, styles.css and analysis.css byte-identical to their recorded pre-experiment hashes. Model provenance text is updated where necessary, while the final canvas and playback stay visually consistent. Browser checks exercise desktop and mobile widths, verify that the graph changes with a changed language and that acceptance changes with the test string. The design goal is stable presentation with correct dynamic content.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('API, Jobs, Caching and Deployment',[
        T(['Route','Role'],[
            ['GET /api/health','Server readiness, active local model and render capability.'],['GET /api/catalog','Examples, model choices and grammar availability.'],['GET /api/research','Measured summaries and experiment metadata.'],['POST /api/jobs','Submit bounded DFA/CFG/compiler/render work.'],['GET /api/jobs/{job_id}','Poll progress, result or explicit error.'],['POST /api/simulate','Recompute a DFA trace from a prior result.'],['GET /api/videos/{name}','Serve an existing validated cache filename.']]),
        'The server uses a bounded ThreadPoolExecutor. Local defaults are two workers and at most four running jobs. Result retention is bounded at 128 entries and job history at 256. Request size, description size and input limits prevent accidental unbounded work. Hosted environment variables can reduce workers and job counts. Parsing uses a configurable subprocess timeout rather than allowing an unbounded grammar request to block indefinitely.',
        'Checkpoint dispatch reads config.kind. A semantic_plan checkpoint loads head.pt and its named encoder; direct checkpoints load weights.pt. A shared encoder cache is keyed by resolved path and file revisions. Grammar and plan heads validate their encoder SHA-256 before use. The active-model pointer is explicit; finding an arbitrary folder does not automatically make it the default. Smoke checkpoints are rejected from the learned product route.',
        'Model-generated language_plan metadata is preserved with a DFA result when available. The trace and diagram still consume the ordinary DFA contract. A reference comparison can select a later candidate, so metadata is associated with the selected candidate rather than blindly reporting the first plan. No model state is stored as a mutable last-plan field shared across concurrent requests.',
        'The local launcher builds the frontend when needed and serves port 8501. An existing build can be reused with --skip-build. Docker and Render manifests describe a deployment configuration using the repository and health endpoint. They do not demonstrate that newly generated local weights have been uploaded or remotely activated. Git ignores generated artifacts and model weights, so a remote deployment requires a separate artifact-provisioning process.',
        'The research endpoint adapts the active model\'s measured original regression result to the existing frontend metric contract and exposes the complete plan comparison separately. This keeps displayed statistics tied to the selected architecture. The UI text identifies the results as a measured regression set rather than continuing to describe a repeatedly inspected set as unseen.',
        'The server treats submitted source and descriptions as data. The arithmetic compiler does not invoke Python eval, shell execution or a generated runtime. The product is a local educational application, not an authenticated multi-tenant inference service. Internet-facing hardening, persistent accounts and distributed job queues are outside the implemented scope.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Grammar Analysis and Checked Derivations',[
        S('BERT Grammar Decoder'),
        'The grammar path uses the evaluated V3 token encoder and a separately trained two-layer cross-attention production decoder. It predicts a finite grammar-token vocabulary and EOS. It does not select a handwritten grammar by matching English text. The head config records vocabulary, sequence length, encoder fingerprint and dataset fingerprint. Invalid grammar output raises an explicit prediction error with the actual candidate productions retained for inspection.',
        'The robust head is trained on 1,652 generated/augmented rows. Its internal validation exactness and generated test reflect canonical production targets, not arbitrary grammar-language equivalence. Known arithmetic forms are intentionally present in training. This makes the compiler workspace reliable for its intended grammar while preventing those fixtures from being advertised as novel held-out discovery.',
        S('Parsing and Evidence'),
        'The formal CFG component parses grammar text, checks declared productions, validates terminals and runs NLTK Earley parsing within a subprocess budget. Returned trees are bounded in count. A tree is checked by matching each expansion to an actual grammar production and comparing the terminal yield with the input token sequence. Epsilon productions are represented explicitly in derivation handling.',
        'A successful tree proves that the supplied token sequence is generated by that predicted grammar. It does not prove that all strings generated by the grammar satisfy the English requirement. General CFG equivalence is not implemented. Bounded challenge membership checks are useful tests, but they must not be labelled a complete equivalence proof.',
        S('Input Dependence and Reuse'),
        'A grammar represents a language, so a change from ab to aabb should not require a different grammar for matching counts. The result cache can reuse BERT productions when description, model checkpoint and head/encoder revision match. It reparses the new token sequence and regenerates its tree layout. A new model revision invalidates the reuse. This saves encoder work while preserving correct input-dependent content.',
        'The parser can reveal several derivations for an ambiguous grammar. The interface lets the user inspect alternatives and expansion order, with a clear bound on returned trees. One returned tree does not establish unambiguity. Arithmetic grammar precedence is supplied by productions, while the ambiguous addition fixture intentionally exposes alternative groupings.',
        S('Grammar Analysis Outputs'),
        'The analysis derives terminal/nonterminal sets, production information, nullable behavior and other inspectable facts from the actual grammar. Tree labels and positions come from returned nodes, not a generic template. Character, token and arithmetic-lexeme modes have different tokenization contracts; a mismatch is an input issue rather than evidence that the model should silently reinterpret source.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Arithmetic Compiler Implementation',[
        S('Supported Language'),
        'The compiler supports symbolic arithmetic expressions with identifiers, numeric literals, parentheses, binary plus/minus/multiplication/division and unary signs. The intended BERT-produced grammar gives multiplication/division higher precedence and binary operators left associativity. Identifiers represent external inputs. The system does not provide statements, declarations, types, loops, functions, control flow, machine code or a runtime interpreter.',
        S('Lexing and Concrete Tokens'),
        'The lexer records token category, original lexeme and source offsets. It distinguishes identifiers, numbers and punctuation, rejects unsupported characters and preserves decimal literals. It accepts at most 4,000 source characters and 64 arithmetic tokens. Source is treated as data. An invalid sequence such as a+*b fails parsing under the actual predicted grammar rather than being fixed into another expression.',
        S('From Parse Tree to AST'),
        'The compiler checks that terminal leaves align exactly with the lexed categories. It maps those leaves back to their original identifier or number lexemes. Unary signs create unary AST nodes. Binary operators create ordered children, and parenthesis productions preserve grouping without becoming redundant AST nodes. Unsupported production shapes raise an error instead of assuming a familiar arithmetic grammar.',
        'For (total + 3) * scale - 4 / 2, the AST must preserve the parenthesized addition, multiplication by scale, division of 4 by 2 and the final subtraction order. This is a structural translation from a checked derivation. It is not a claim that the system infers arbitrary compiler semantics from a broad English programming-language specification.',
        S('Intermediate Representation and Independent Check'),
        'Postorder lowering produces temporaries t0, t1 and subsequent targets. Each instruction records an operator and ordered operands, which may be identifiers, numbers or earlier temporaries. The result identifies the final value. An independent reconstruction checks temporary-before-definition errors, operator arity and operand order, then compares the reconstructed symbolic expression with the AST expression. This catches lost or reordered subexpressions in lowering.',
        'The compiler view displays tokens, AST and instruction sequence derived from the current source. Changing source alters these structures. A simple leaf-only expression can have no intermediate instructions and no expansion animation steps; that is valid behavior, not a missing diagram. Division is kept symbolic, so runtime division-by-zero behavior is outside the implemented guarantee.',
        S('Evaluation Scope'),
        'Compiler challenge success counts both valid-source acceptance and correct rejection of intentionally invalid source under the desired grammar. The nine-case mixed test is too small to establish broad compiler correctness. Unit tests cover precedence, associativity, unary operators, lexical failure and IR consistency. Further language features require new specifications, BERT supervision and formal checking contracts rather than another generic tree.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Evidence Boundaries and Rendering',[
        S('Three Different Claims'),
        'Structure checked means a supplied or predicted DFA has a complete valid transition table, or a grammar/tree conforms to its explicit contract. Simulated means a trace follows that object for one input. Verified equivalence means the candidate DFA matches an independent reference for every word over the matching alphabet. These claims have different evidence requirements and remain distinct in result records.',
        'An arbitrary English request normally lacks an independent formal reference. The application can show an unverified candidate preview and its trace. That view is useful for inspection and debugging, but the system does not relabel it as exact English correctness. Manual input is also clearly labelled and does not count as BERT prediction.',
        S('Sealed Export Objects'),
        'The DFA verification gate issues a sealed candidate/reference pair only after exact equivalence. The renderer accepts this evidence object rather than an arbitrary candidate dictionary. It rechecks the seal and equivalence, then reconstructs the requested trace. Tree exports likewise require production and yield checks. The contract prevents a rejected neural prediction from receiving an authoritative-looking verified video.',
        'An incorrect candidate is never replaced by a known constructor output in the learned route. Tests deliberately supply a wrong BERT candidate for a known suffix benchmark and verify that the equivalence failure remains visible. Reference demonstrations are separately labelled, so using them is transparent rather than inflating learned accuracy.',
        S('Dynamic Visual Content'),
        'Graph layout derives nodes from actual states and edges from actual transitions. Start arrows, self-loops, reciprocal transitions and accepting rings reflect that structure. Trace frames derive the current state from simulation. Parse layout derives nodes and links from actual derivations. AST views derive their nodes from compiler lowering input. The visual system is reusable, while mathematical content is recomputed for each relevant request.',
        'The new experiment records hashes for six presentation files and confirms that all remain unchanged. Thus the final visualization style is preserved. Browser and HTTP checks separately verify the active model, graph counts, changing trace acceptance, grammar tree growth and compiler behavior. A source hash alone would not prove correct runtime integration.',
        S('Caching and Export Limits'),
        'Video cache keys depend on the validated payload and rendering options. A cache hit reuses a previously created file for the same evidence/input contract. Manim is optional and guarded by availability. Large graphs or trees may exceed readable rendering budgets even when formal checks succeed; the result retains the formal object and reports a visual limit rather than silently drawing an unrelated simplified structure.'
    ]))
    verification=read('artifacts/plan-verification.json') if (ROOT/'artifacts/plan-verification.json').exists() else {}
    pages.append(('Testing and Integration Verification',[
        S('Formal and Neural-Decoder Tests'),
        'New predicate-based compiler tests enumerate binary words through length six and compare simulation against independent Python predicates for conjunction, union, negation, exact counts, count bounds, count modulo and total-length conditions. They cover empty input and overlapping literal patterns. Invalid modulus, invalid patterns, Boolean-as-integer arguments and state-budget violations are rejected.',
        'A decoder test injects ideal logits for a composite plan with literals 00101 and 10. It verifies that learned-copy mechanics preserve leading zeros, respect full literal boundaries and reconstruct the intended typed tree. This tests the decoder independently of neural training quality. Dataset tests verify one partition per canonical language and no training-language overlap with the frozen comparison.',
        S('Application Regression Tests'),
        'Existing tests cover invalid structure, exact equivalence and shortest counterexamples, strict JSON, unverified manual previews, smoke-checkpoint rejection, no rule fallback, grammar reuse revision checks and compiler failures retaining actual productions. Head-checkpoint integration is checked through the same learned route used by the product. The full suite is rerun after final code changes, with results saved in the verification artifact.',
        S('Runtime Checks'),
        'The local server is restarted with the updated model dispatcher. HTTP checks exercise asynchronous jobs, independent-reference rejection, valid derivations, compiler tasks and available video export. Browser checks run the real React UI on desktop and mobile. They confirm suffix-01 produces three states, suffix-0101 produces five, a changed input changes acceptance, grammar inputs alter parse-tree size and compiler output follows source.',
        'The six frozen presentation files are SHA-256 compared against their pre-experiment values. These checks protect the requested visualization presentation while model code and provenance copy change. Browser console errors and responsive overflow are checked separately; a passing Python suite alone would not catch a broken frontend contract.',
        S('Recorded Verification Status'),
        f"The final verification record reports: {verification.get('summary','Final verification is recorded alongside the completed reports in artifacts/plan-verification.json.')} Results are integration evidence for the supported application paths. They do not convert a finite regression suite into a proof of unrestricted English interpretation.",
        'The research comparison remains separate from software tests. Model semantic scores come from independently generated reference DFAs on the frozen cases. Software tests validate contracts and implementation behavior. Both are necessary, but neither should be substituted for the other in a project presentation.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Reproducibility and Local Operation',[
        S('Starting the Existing Local Installation'),
        '<font name="Courier" size="9">.venv/bin/python scripts/run_local.py --port 8501</font>',
        'Open http://localhost:8501 after the server reports readiness. The launcher builds the frontend unless --skip-build is supplied. For an already built frontend, use .venv/bin/python scripts/run_local.py --port 8501 --skip-build. The Python environment must include the project dependencies, and Node is needed for rebuilding React. The project source configuration accepts Python 3.10 or later; the recorded local interpreter is Python 3.12.',
        S('Data and Model Reproduction'),
        '<font name="Courier" size="8.5">.venv/bin/python scripts/download_training_assets.py<br/>.venv/bin/python -m theorybert.data.prepare --help<br/>.venv/bin/python -m theorybert.data.expand --help<br/>.venv/bin/python -m theorybert.data.plans</font>',
        'Review the preparation and expansion CLI options before rerunning corpus generation. The plan builder intentionally refuses to overwrite an existing frozen v2 comparison. For an independent later experiment, introduce a new version and freeze it before predictions. Reproducing the plan head requires the exact evaluated V3 encoder, not merely a fresh pretrained BERT download.',
        '<font name="Courier" size="8.5">.venv/bin/python -m theorybert.models.semantic --epochs 60<br/>.venv/bin/python scripts/compare_plan_model.py<br/>.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q</font>',
        'The comparison script refuses to overwrite an existing selection. It is an audit boundary, not a command to repeatedly tune the same test. A clean reproduction should use isolated output paths and retain the protocol, dataset fingerprints and encoder artifact. Training is CPU-limited by code and can stop if resource thresholds are exceeded. Do not launch another training job alongside it on this machine.',
        S('Selecting or Rolling Back a Model'),
        f'The active pointer currently selects {final}. The previous direct checkpoint remains artifacts/bert-v3/best. Settings can select a checkpoint explicitly for inspection. A default rollback should update artifacts/active-model.json to an evaluated compatible checkpoint and its measured summary paths. The plan head depends on its recorded V3 encoder; deleting that encoder breaks both plan and grammar loading.',
        S('Artifact Availability and Reporting'),
        'Model weights, generated datasets and evaluation directories are local and ignored by Git. The repository URL identifies the project, not guaranteed access to this working-tree snapshot. A shareable research release should include a source commit, dependency lock, permitted datasets or deterministic generation instructions, source/license notices, checkpoint checksums and evaluation outputs. The report builder is stored under docs/reports/ and reads the completed local experiment evidence.',
        'The two PDFs can be regenerated with .venv/bin/python docs/reports/build_reports.py after its report dependencies are available. They use embedded Times New Roman fonts and vector architecture diagrams. Their page renderings and text checks are retained as local QA artifacts.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Limitations and Failure Analysis',[
        S('Language Understanding'),
        'The new model remains imperfect on the fresh test despite large gains over V3. Typed validity does not eliminate operator confusion or numeric mistakes. Negation scope, multiple literals, inclusive/exclusive bounds, ambiguous descriptions and unseen terminology can cause incorrect plans. The model has no calibrated guarantee that a high-scoring output matches the English. A valid output can therefore be confidently wrong.',
        'The task is binary and the plan vocabulary is finite. Requests for arbitrary alphabets, nonregular conditions, unrestricted long-count values or complex Boolean nesting are outside demonstrated coverage. The current implementation can still produce a valid in-scope interpretation for an out-of-scope request, so unsupported-intent detection is a research need. Type checks only validate the predicted object; they do not understand whether the request should have been rejected.',
        S('Evaluation and Generalization'),
        'The comparison uses generated descriptions and one seed. It shares operator families with training and combines an architectural change with more supervision. Fresh test size is 120, with only 57 matched cases. The legacy regression test and retired challenge have been inspected repeatedly. These factors prevent broad claims about unrestricted user language, isolated architectural causality or statistically stable superiority across runs.',
        'The all-case fresh score includes 63 capacity-mismatched cases. It is useful for system coverage, but the matched subset is the appropriate direct-head comparison. Within-five success depends on a reference and cannot be promised for arbitrary free-form English. A general correctness percentage for the entire product would mix incompatible tasks and evidence types.',
        S('Grammar and Compiler Scope'),
        'Production exactness is sensitive to grammar spelling and order conventions. Alternative equivalent grammars may score differently, and general CFG equivalence is not checked. A successful derivation only proves membership under the predicted productions. Compiler support is limited to symbolic arithmetic and does not include a runtime, optimizer, machine backend, types or full language specification. Correct rejection is counted explicitly in negative compiler fixtures.',
        S('Performance and Operational Risks'),
        'The head is much smaller to train, but BERT remains the inference backbone and the new plan compiler adds work. The measured response-time cost is real. Resource guards use periodic snapshots and cannot prevent every brief pressure spike or unrelated background disk change. Historical checkpoints already consume substantial local storage, so another experiment should first budget its exact artifact requirements. No automatic deletion policy is introduced.',
        'There has been no controlled educational user study, accessibility audit or internet-scale load test. The interface may help inspect formal behavior, but improved learning outcomes are unmeasured. These are future validation tasks rather than implied benefits of a visually polished system.'
    ]))
    pages.append(('Contributions and Development Roadmap',[
        S('Implemented Contributions'),
        'The project combines BERT-based English interpretation with exact formal execution and provenance-aware educational visualization. It provides a direct automata predictor, a learned CFG production decoder, an arithmetic AST/IR path and a new compact plan decoder with learned literal copying. The same final diagram system consumes the formal result, preserving the requested presentation while allowing its content to change correctly.',
        'It also contributes an auditable local workflow: canonical-language splits, pinned public inputs, documented filtering, versioned generated supervision, validation-only deployment gates, retained negative results and resource logs. These are practical project contributions. They build on prior semantic parsing, intermediate representations, formal constraints and classical language theory.',
        S('Immediate Next Work'),
        'The highest-value next step is a new independent human-authored evaluation set with ambiguous requests, minimal semantic edits and explicit unsupported cases. It should be frozen before model changes and include pairings such as starts versus ends, contains versus avoids, at most versus at least and one literal substitution with leading zeros. Errors should be categorized by operator, numeric argument, literal selection, composition and formal-capacity rejection.',
        'A matched-data experiment should train direct and plan heads with the same eligible supervision and comparable encoder conditions. At least three seeds would reveal variance. Additional controls should isolate learned copying, typed constraints, Boolean compositions and teacher-label choice. The current experiment evaluates the complete approach; these controls would identify which parts produce the gains.',
        S('Model and Product Improvements'),
        'Confidence calibration and explicit unsupported-request classification should precede a claim of dependable open-ended use. A model-produced plan could be shown in an editable confirmation field, allowing users to correct intent before construction. This would preserve the final visualization while making semantic ambiguity visible. It should be evaluated as a human-in-the-loop system, not counted as unaided model accuracy.',
        'Performance work can profile encoder time, prefix search and formal construction separately. Safe memoization of compatible immutable results may reduce repeated work. Quantization or a smaller encoder should be accepted only after semantic and grammar regression checks. New alphabets, richer grammars and compiler statements require expanded data and new formal contracts, rather than merely increasing a maximum length.',
        S('Research Release Readiness'),
        'Before external submission, add the actual author/institution block, select a target venue, apply its exact official template and verify page/margin requirements. Release permitted data artifacts or exact generation recipes, cite source notices and archive the trained head plus encoder lineage. The eight-page companion is an IEEE-style project manuscript; this comprehensive report provides the operational evidence and limitations needed to assess it.'
    ]))
    # Dataset registry includes the actual artifact-generated grammar corpora.
    inventory=read('artifacts/report-data-inventory.json')
    for relative in ['artifacts/bert-grammar-pilot/dataset.jsonl','artifacts/bert-grammar/dataset.jsonl','artifacts/grammar-robust/dataset.jsonl']:
        path=ROOT/relative
        rows=[json.loads(l) for l in path.read_text().splitlines()]
        inventory.append({'path':relative,'examples':len(rows),'sha256':hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'splits':dict(Counter(r['split'] for r in rows))})
    registry=[]
